0.25.0
 - date: 06-11-2026
 - changes:
   - feat: Razorpay billing: monthly subscriptions and yearly orders, with the signed webhook (`/api/v1/webhooks/razorpay`) as the source of truth. Events are idempotent (unique event id), applied under a row lock, ignored when older than the last applied one, and amounts are checked against the plan price
   - feat: no paying twice: a live monthly subscription or a paid-up yearly plan can't be bought again (409 with the date renewal opens); yearly renewal opens 7 days before the end
   - feat: payment receipts (proof of payment, not a GST invoice): `GET /billing/receipts` and `/billing/receipts/{payment_id}`, numbered `RV8-{year}-{seq}`, one per captured payment, never twice for the same payment. Issuer name and address come from `RECEIPT_ISSUER_NAME` and `RECEIPT_ISSUER_ADDRESS`
   - feat: change plan like a subscription product: `GET /billing/change-plan/quote` and `POST /billing/change-plan`. Upgrade is immediate (yearly: pay the prorated difference, end date unchanged; monthly: new subscription starts today and the old one is cancelled once it charges). Downgrade takes effect at the period end (monthly: a scheduled subscription; yearly: remembered and applied on renewal). A cancelled monthly plan can be resumed. The old subscription is always cancelled by the new one, never edited, because Razorpay can't update a UPI subscription
   - feat: the plan follows the payment straight away: after the checkout signature is verified, `/billing/verify` reads the payment, subscription or order from Razorpay's API and applies it through the same code as the webhook, so a late or unreachable webhook no longer leaves the customer on Free; the webhook arriving later changes nothing
   - feat: `billing status` also returns `can_buy`, `renewal_opens_at`, `can_change_plan`, `pending_plan`, `pending_plan_name` and `pending_change_on`; admin-granted plans show their expiry as the period end
   - fix: a platform admin can't change the plan of a business under a live monthly subscription (409), so the admin screen and billing can't disagree
   - config: `RAZORPAY_KEY_ID`, `RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET`, `RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, `RECEIPT_ISSUER_NAME`, `RECEIPT_ISSUER_ADDRESS`. Webhook events to enable: `subscription.*` (including `subscription.authenticated`), `order.paid` and `payment.failed`
   - migration 0059 to 0062: billing columns, payment events, `payments` table with its receipt sequence and row-level security, and `pending_plan_slug`, `pending_subscription_id` and `pending_kind` on businesses; run `alembic upgrade head` before deploying this version
   - test: webhook signature, idempotency, stale and out-of-order events, amount checks, receipts, every plan-change path (upgrade, downgrade, resume, keep, refusals), and confirming a checkout without the webhook (monthly, yearly, unpaid, underpaid, other business, Razorpay outage)
   - note: refunds are only logged, receipts aren't emailed, and GST invoices aren't built; the plan-change and confirm-from-Razorpay flows haven't been run against Razorpay's API yet, only against mocks

0.24.0
 - date: 05-11-2026
 - changes:
   - feat: email invites replace handed-over passwords. Creating a business admin or a team member with no password emails a one-time link (`/accept-invite`) where they confirm their email and set their own password; an invited account can't sign in until then. With no SMTP configured nothing changes: a temporary password is generated and returned once, and an explicit password still works
   - feat: `POST /auth/accept-invite` (public): consumes the token, sets the password, marks the email verified and clears `must_change_password`. Only a SHA-256 of the token is stored; a link works once, expires after `INVITE_TTL_HOURS` (default 72), and a newer invite replaces the older link. Dead, expired and unknown links all return the same 400
   - feat: two emails. Platform admin creates a business (or re-sends to its admin): a Reve8 welcome sent as the platform, with product highlights and a footer (company, website, support). A studio admin adds a member: "{studio} invites you to join the team" sent under the studio's name with Reply-To set to the inviter (the business contact when the platform admin did). HTML plus plain text; business names and links are escaped
   - feat: the sender address stays the verified `SMTP_FROM`; only the display name changes (sending as another domain would fail SPF/DKIM). A platform display name comes from `SMTP_FROM`, e.g. `Reve8 Pvt Ltd <hi@reve8.com>`
   - feat: `POST /businesses/{id}/resend-invite` (platform admin): re-sends the welcome to the business admin who hasn't set up yet; 409 if they already have, so a working admin's password can't be reset by mistake. Returns the temporary password instead when email isn't configured
   - feat: team create returns `invite_sent` and `temporary_password` (shown once, never an explicit password); reset-password returns `invite_sent` and `temporary_password`; `password` is optional on team create; members expose `invite_pending`; `/auth/me` returns `invites_enabled`
   - feat: audit: `team.member_added` and `team.password_reset` record `invited`, and accepting is logged as `auth.invite_accepted` (no tokens in the log)
   - feat: a mail failure is a 502 with a generic message (no provider details) and no half-created account
   - config: `SMTP_HOST`, `SMTP_PORT` (587 STARTTLS, 465 implicit TLS), `SMTP_USER`, `SMTP_PASSWORD`, `SMTP_FROM`, `SMTP_STARTTLS`, `INVITE_TTL_HOURS`; email is on only when both `SMTP_HOST` and `SMTP_FROM` are set. Email footers reuse existing settings: company name and contact address come from `SMTP_FROM` (e.g. `Reve8 Pvt Ltd <hi@reve8.com>`) and the website link from `FRONTEND_URL`
   - migration 0058: `users.email_verified_at`, `invite_token_hash` (indexed) and `invite_expires_at`, all nullable; run it before deploying this version or login fails on the missing columns
   - test: SMTP transport (STARTTLS, implicit TLS, login, headers, multipart, failures never leak details), sender-name handling and header-injection safety, both email templates (wording, footer, escaping, links, expiry), token hashing, expiry, single use and re-issue, every create/accept/resend path including disabled and removed members, business create and business-level resend, and `/auth/me`. Tests force SMTP off so they never send real mail
   - note: sending as a studio's own address would need each studio to verify its own domain with the mail provider; not included

